      "level": "CLASS",
      "where": "SKILL.md Step 4 vs evals.json eval 4 (E1, E2) and eval 4's prompt",
      "finding": "Step 4 demotes AUROC and makes the gate decidable only against the precision target. Eval 4's prompt supplies no target, and step 1 forbids the run from picking one ('Neither number is yours to pick'), so the gate as written is unrunnable there. What the eval actually grades is the AUROC/flat-bucket evidence the step says does not decide the gate. The step and its only reject-case expectation grade different things.",
      "quote": "AUROC ranks the candidates. It does not decide the gate ... The decidable form of the gate is the target itself. // E1 reports a number showing the signal does not separate - AUROC near 0.5, a correlation near zero, or a flat or non-monotone bucket table"
    },
    {
      "level": "CLASS",
      "where": "evals/evals.json coverage of SKILL.md steps 2, 7, 8, 9 and of assets/threshold-decision-record.md",
      "finding": "Four of ten steps end in a checkable produce that no expectation grades: step 2 (plural candidates, 2-3 carried forward), step 7 (named receiver, capacity, headroom), step 8 (per-slice table), step 9 (band plus re-check date). The whole post-cut half of the procedure is ungraded, and no eval supplies slices to miss or a re-validation trigger. Worse, the artefact declares the filled record to be the output, and not one of the 17 expectations across four cases mentions the record or any of its sections - every expectation grades prose numbers.",
      "quote": "The output is a filled decision record, not a recommendation in prose ... A step is done when its slot in the record holds a number or a name, not when it has been thought about."
    },
    {
      "level": "CLASS",
      "where": "SKILL.md Step 7 provenance note vs Step 8 provenance note and Step 9",
      "finding": "Step 7's parenthetical asserts a property of the whole file that the next step denies two paragraphs later. Step 8 admits it comes from a measured claim rather than an observed failure; step 9 states no provenance at all and is likewise backed only by C6. So at least three steps are rules with no observed failure behind them, and the body's own claim that 'the rest of this file traces to observed failures' is false as written.",
      "quote": "Provenance, stated because the rest of this file traces to observed failures and this step does not // Provenance: this step comes from a measured claim rather than from an observed failure - no baseline run was given slices to miss. It is the one step this build has not seen fail."
    },
    {
      "level": "INSTANCE",
      "where": "evals/evals.json eval 2, expected_output and E2, against evals/scored_predictions.csv",
      "finding": "The accepted band admits cuts that miss the very target the case sets. Computed on the fixture: cut 0.503 gives 94.69 percent precision on 113 answered, cut 0.504 gives 94.64 percent on 112 - both inside the stated 0.503 to 0.510 band, both below the 95 percent target. The band was drawn as one answered case in score space and crossed the target boundary while doing it, so the eval grades as correct an answer step 6 and step 1 forbid.",
      "quote": "the RECOMMENDED cut is score >= 0.506, or a value within one answered case of it (0.503 to 0.510) // Once agreed it is binding - step 6 does not trade precision away to buy coverage, and a cut that misses the target escalates rather than settling."
